1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,390
Twenty point five million dollars.

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:05,356
That is how much each big college

3
00:00:05,356 --> 00:00:08,469
athletic department was allowed to pay its

4
00:00:08,469 --> 00:00:10,361
own athletes directly,

5
00:00:10,361 --> 00:00:12,277
in the school year that started in

6
00:00:12,277 --> 00:00:14,075
twenty twenty-five.

7
00:00:14,317 --> 00:00:16,449
For more than a century,

8
00:00:16,449 --> 00:00:19,207
college sports ran on one idea.

9
00:00:19,337 --> 00:00:20,944
The players were amateurs,

10
00:00:20,944 --> 00:00:22,575
and their schools could not pay them

11
00:00:22,575 --> 00:00:24,107
a salary.

12
00:00:24,347 --> 00:00:25,537
In its first year,

13
00:00:25,537 --> 00:00:27,722
the new system paid out about one

14
00:00:27,722 --> 00:00:30,150
point seven seven billion dollars to

15
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:31,478
athletes.

16
00:00:31,717 --> 00:00:34,571
So how did college sports suddenly get

17
00:00:34,571 --> 00:00:36,027
a payroll?

18
00:00:36,207 --> 00:00:38,566
And who ends up paying for it?

19
00:00:38,817 --> 00:00:41,040
By the end of this video,

20
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:43,009
you will know how a court case

21
00:00:43,009 --> 00:00:44,669
created this payroll,

22
00:00:44,669 --> 00:00:46,610
how the money is split,

23
00:00:46,610 --> 00:00:47,988
who checks the deals,

24
00:00:47,988 --> 00:00:50,239
and why some smaller sports are paying

25
00:00:50,239 --> 00:00:51,402
the price.

26
00:00:57,050 --> 00:00:59,528
It started with a class action by

27
00:00:59,528 --> 00:01:02,558
college athletes against the NCAA and the

28
00:01:02,558 --> 00:01:04,810
biggest conferences.

29
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:07,038
The case was about money from the

30
00:01:07,038 --> 00:01:08,282
athletes' names,

31
00:01:08,282 --> 00:01:10,158
images and likenesses,

32
00:01:10,158 --> 00:01:12,477
including the television deals built on

33
00:01:12,477 --> 00:01:13,710
their games.

34
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:15,397
Most of the damages,

35
00:01:15,397 --> 00:01:17,157
about two billion dollars,

36
00:01:17,157 --> 00:01:19,362
were set aside for exactly that,

37
00:01:19,362 --> 00:01:21,589
the athletes' share of name and image

38
00:01:21,589 --> 00:01:23,303
money from the past.

39
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:25,397
In June twenty twenty-five,

40
00:01:25,397 --> 00:01:27,969
federal judge Claudia Wilken gave final

41
00:01:27,969 --> 00:01:30,440
approval to a settlement.

42
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:33,355
The back damages alone came to about

43
00:01:33,355 --> 00:01:35,254
two point six billion dollars,

44
00:01:35,254 --> 00:01:37,659
to be paid every year over ten

45
00:01:37,659 --> 00:01:38,115
years,

46
00:01:38,115 --> 00:01:40,279
to a class of almost three hundred

47
00:01:40,279 --> 00:01:42,924
and ninety thousand current and former

48
00:01:42,924 --> 00:01:44,245
athletes.

49
00:01:44,483 --> 00:01:46,827
But the real change was about the

50
00:01:46,827 --> 00:01:48,013
future.

51
00:01:48,103 --> 00:01:50,151
Schools may now share revenue directly

52
00:01:50,151 --> 00:01:51,493
with their athletes,

53
00:01:51,493 --> 00:01:54,223
up to twenty-two percent of the average

54
00:01:54,223 --> 00:01:56,271
athletic revenue of the biggest

55
00:01:56,271 --> 00:01:57,385
conferences,

56
00:01:57,385 --> 00:02:00,033
with set increases every year.

57
00:02:00,283 --> 00:02:01,648
In the first year,

58
00:02:01,648 --> 00:02:04,154
that cap was twenty point five million

59
00:02:04,154 --> 00:02:05,868
dollars per school.

60
00:02:06,117 --> 00:02:08,487
And it keeps rising.

61
00:02:08,577 --> 00:02:11,071
For the twenty twenty-six season,

62
00:02:11,071 --> 00:02:13,086
the cap went up to twenty-one point

63
00:02:13,086 --> 00:02:15,197
five eight million dollars.

64
00:02:15,297 --> 00:02:17,701
The plaintiffs' own expert estimated that

65
00:02:17,701 --> 00:02:19,865
by the middle of the next decade,

66
00:02:19,865 --> 00:02:21,833
it could approach thirty-three million

67
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:23,307
dollars per school.

68
00:02:23,337 --> 00:02:24,871
That is an estimate,

69
00:02:24,871 --> 00:02:26,360
not a promise.

70
00:02:26,617 --> 00:02:28,637
Let me draw the pie.

71
00:02:28,647 --> 00:02:31,277
Here is a big conference school's athletic

72
00:02:31,277 --> 00:02:31,970
revenue,

73
00:02:31,970 --> 00:02:33,287
one circle.

74
00:02:33,387 --> 00:02:34,772
Television money,

75
00:02:34,772 --> 00:02:35,355
tickets,

76
00:02:35,355 --> 00:02:36,437
donations.

77
00:02:36,437 --> 00:02:37,676
Now cut a slice,

78
00:02:37,676 --> 00:02:40,007
up to twenty-two percent.

79
00:02:40,007 --> 00:02:42,437
That slice is the payroll.

80
00:02:42,547 --> 00:02:44,412
The rest still has to pay for

81
00:02:44,412 --> 00:02:45,185
coaches,

82
00:02:45,185 --> 00:02:45,957
stadiums,

83
00:02:45,957 --> 00:02:48,955
travel and every other team.

84
00:02:49,217 --> 00:02:51,399
So where did that money go in

85
00:02:51,399 --> 00:02:52,817
the first year?

86
00:02:52,827 --> 00:02:55,588
The new regulator of college sports has

87
00:02:55,588 --> 00:02:56,941
the numbers.

88
00:03:07,583 --> 00:03:09,431
The College Sports Commission,

89
00:03:09,431 --> 00:03:11,747
created to police the new system,

90
00:03:11,747 --> 00:03:14,553
reported its first-year results in October

91
00:03:14,553 --> 00:03:16,323
twenty twenty-six.

92
00:03:16,463 --> 00:03:18,978
Schools paid about one point seven seven

93
00:03:18,978 --> 00:03:21,468
billion dollars in revenue sharing,

94
00:03:21,468 --> 00:03:24,461
to almost thirty-five thousand athletes,

95
00:03:24,461 --> 00:03:27,073
at three hundred and seven schools.

96
00:03:27,317 --> 00:03:29,953
Counting everything the commission tracks,

97
00:03:29,953 --> 00:03:32,390
including scholarships and education

98
00:03:32,390 --> 00:03:33,033
awards,

99
00:03:33,033 --> 00:03:35,470
payments to athletes reached about one

100
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:37,464
point nine eight billion dollars in that

101
00:03:37,464 --> 00:03:38,507
first year.

102
00:03:38,767 --> 00:03:39,685
For context,

103
00:03:39,685 --> 00:03:41,568
that is about the same as the

104
00:03:41,568 --> 00:03:44,157
entire ten-year damages fund for name and

105
00:03:44,157 --> 00:03:45,075
image rights,

106
00:03:45,075 --> 00:03:47,558
paid out in a single season.

107
00:03:47,817 --> 00:03:50,667
Not every school went all in.

108
00:03:50,817 --> 00:03:53,106
Of the three hundred and nineteen schools

109
00:03:53,106 --> 00:03:55,143
that opted into the system,

110
00:03:55,143 --> 00:03:57,203
sixty-eight hit the cap or came within

111
00:03:57,203 --> 00:03:58,947
five percent of it.

112
00:03:59,127 --> 00:04:01,111
Most schools in the system paid less

113
00:04:01,111 --> 00:04:02,575
than the maximum,

114
00:04:02,575 --> 00:04:04,807
because most schools simply do not have

115
00:04:04,807 --> 00:04:06,648
that kind of money.

116
00:04:06,883 --> 00:04:09,883
That television money is huge.

117
00:04:09,883 --> 00:04:12,121
The Southeastern Conference alone

118
00:04:12,121 --> 00:04:14,857
distributed one point zero three billion

119
00:04:14,857 --> 00:04:17,095
dollars to its sixteen schools for the

120
00:04:17,095 --> 00:04:19,933
twenty twenty-four twenty-five year.

121
00:04:20,063 --> 00:04:22,010
For a school in a conference like

122
00:04:22,010 --> 00:04:22,421
that,

123
00:04:22,421 --> 00:04:24,801
a twenty million dollar payroll is a

124
00:04:24,801 --> 00:04:25,429
big bill,

125
00:04:25,429 --> 00:04:27,111
but a payable one.

126
00:04:27,350 --> 00:04:30,230
Look at one of the richest programs.

127
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:32,760
Ohio State reported record athletic

128
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:34,789
revenue of about three hundred and

129
00:04:34,789 --> 00:04:37,268
thirty-six million dollars in fiscal year

130
00:04:37,268 --> 00:04:38,598
twenty twenty-five,

131
00:04:38,598 --> 00:04:41,078
and expenses of about three hundred and

132
00:04:41,078 --> 00:04:42,580
twenty million.

133
00:04:42,710 --> 00:04:44,165
By our calculation,

134
00:04:44,165 --> 00:04:46,274
the difference was about fifteen point

135
00:04:46,274 --> 00:04:48,120
seven million dollars.

136
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:50,822
Less than one year's payroll cap.

137
00:04:51,083 --> 00:04:53,293
Let me draw the budget.

138
00:04:53,303 --> 00:04:55,233
Here is the revenue bar.

139
00:04:55,363 --> 00:04:57,211
Here is the expense bar,

140
00:04:57,211 --> 00:04:58,883
almost as tall.

141
00:04:58,933 --> 00:05:01,453
The gap between them is thin.

142
00:05:01,533 --> 00:05:03,953
Now add a twenty million dollar payroll

143
00:05:03,953 --> 00:05:06,093
on top of the expenses.

144
00:05:06,233 --> 00:05:08,495
Even a record year does not cover

145
00:05:08,495 --> 00:05:11,131
it without changes somewhere else.

146
00:05:11,383 --> 00:05:14,053
And changes did come.

147
00:05:14,173 --> 00:05:17,672
Starting with the size of every team.

148
00:05:23,317 --> 00:05:25,817
The old system limited how many

149
00:05:25,817 --> 00:05:28,167
scholarships a team could give.

150
00:05:28,277 --> 00:05:30,568
The new one limits how many athletes

151
00:05:30,568 --> 00:05:32,417
a team can have at all.

152
00:05:32,687 --> 00:05:35,420
Football rosters are capped at one hundred

153
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:36,517
and five.

154
00:05:36,687 --> 00:05:39,087
Basketball at fifteen.

155
00:05:39,207 --> 00:05:40,117
Baseball at thirty-four.

156
00:05:40,117 --> 00:05:41,607
Softball at twenty-five.

157
00:05:41,617 --> 00:05:44,117
Golf at nine.

158
00:05:44,117 --> 00:05:46,437
The plaintiffs' expert estimated that

159
00:05:46,437 --> 00:05:48,757
removing the old scholarship caps could

160
00:05:48,757 --> 00:05:51,077
open more than one hundred and fifteen

161
00:05:51,077 --> 00:05:53,165
thousand new scholarships across the

162
00:05:53,165 --> 00:05:53,837
country,

163
00:05:53,837 --> 00:05:55,438
another estimate,

164
00:05:55,438 --> 00:05:56,734
not a count.

165
00:05:56,983 --> 00:05:59,184
Any school may now give a scholarship

166
00:05:59,184 --> 00:06:01,583
to every athlete on its roster,

167
00:06:01,583 --> 00:06:03,503
if it can afford to.

168
00:06:03,743 --> 00:06:04,984
But for walk-ons,

169
00:06:04,984 --> 00:06:07,011
the players who joined teams without

170
00:06:07,011 --> 00:06:07,999
scholarships,

171
00:06:07,999 --> 00:06:10,280
the new limits could mean losing a

172
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:11,133
spot.

173
00:06:11,313 --> 00:06:12,841
Athletes who were cut,

174
00:06:12,841 --> 00:06:13,926
or would have been,

175
00:06:13,926 --> 00:06:15,919
are exempt from the limits for the

176
00:06:15,919 --> 00:06:18,069
rest of their college careers.

177
00:06:18,317 --> 00:06:19,607
Some schools cut whole sports.

178
00:06:19,607 --> 00:06:21,487
Stephen F.

179
00:06:21,607 --> 00:06:24,571
Austin State University in Texas dropped

180
00:06:24,571 --> 00:06:25,233
bowling,

181
00:06:25,233 --> 00:06:27,457
both golf teams and beach volleyball.

182
00:06:27,457 --> 00:06:30,516
The university said it was responding to

183
00:06:30,516 --> 00:06:33,576
the anticipated financial impact of the

184
00:06:33,576 --> 00:06:36,765
coming revenue-sharing requirements.

185
00:06:37,017 --> 00:06:39,727
That raises a legal question.

186
00:06:39,807 --> 00:06:40,668
Title IX,

187
00:06:40,668 --> 00:06:43,539
the federal law against sex discrimination

188
00:06:43,539 --> 00:06:44,842
in education,

189
00:06:44,842 --> 00:06:46,687
applies to college sports.

190
00:06:46,687 --> 00:06:48,461
The judge said schools are free to

191
00:06:48,461 --> 00:06:50,743
divide the money in a way that

192
00:06:50,743 --> 00:06:52,492
complies with Title IX,

193
00:06:52,492 --> 00:06:55,280
and the settlement does not release Title

194
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:56,327
IX claims.

195
00:06:56,327 --> 00:06:59,642
Lawsuits over the split are still pending.

196
00:06:59,883 --> 00:07:01,603
Let me draw the bench.

197
00:07:01,603 --> 00:07:03,427
Here is a football team,

198
00:07:03,427 --> 00:07:05,613
one hundred and five seats.

199
00:07:05,663 --> 00:07:06,343
Before,

200
00:07:06,343 --> 00:07:08,194
the roster could be bigger,

201
00:07:08,194 --> 00:07:10,069
with walk-ons at the end of the

202
00:07:10,069 --> 00:07:10,903
bench.

203
00:07:11,023 --> 00:07:13,373
Now the bench has a hard edge.

204
00:07:13,373 --> 00:07:14,716
And next to it,

205
00:07:14,716 --> 00:07:16,060
the smaller sports,

206
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:18,304
some with an empty bench.

207
00:07:18,550 --> 00:07:20,677
There is one more source of money

208
00:07:20,677 --> 00:07:22,340
for athletes.

209
00:07:22,620 --> 00:07:24,887
And it has its own referee.

210
00:07:30,550 --> 00:07:33,240
Athletes can still sign deals with outside

211
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:35,097
companies for their name,

212
00:07:35,097 --> 00:07:36,953
image and likeness,

213
00:07:36,953 --> 00:07:38,360
known as NIL.

214
00:07:38,370 --> 00:07:40,569
But now any outside deal worth six

215
00:07:40,569 --> 00:07:43,256
hundred dollars or more is reviewed by

216
00:07:43,256 --> 00:07:45,810
a clearinghouse called NIL Go.

217
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:47,586
It checks whether the deal has a

218
00:07:47,586 --> 00:07:49,152
valid business purpose,

219
00:07:49,152 --> 00:07:50,739
and whether the pay is in a

220
00:07:50,739 --> 00:07:52,330
reasonable range.

221
00:07:52,583 --> 00:07:54,893
The results are public.

222
00:07:54,893 --> 00:07:57,179
Through October twenty twenty-six,

223
00:07:57,179 --> 00:07:59,258
more than fifty-six thousand deals were

224
00:07:59,258 --> 00:07:59,928
cleared,

225
00:07:59,928 --> 00:08:02,699
worth about seven hundred and seventy-five

226
00:08:02,699 --> 00:08:04,223
million dollars.

227
00:08:04,473 --> 00:08:06,201
And two thousand eight hundred and

228
00:08:06,201 --> 00:08:08,554
seventy-seven deals were not cleared,

229
00:08:08,554 --> 00:08:11,146
worth about one hundred and seventy-seven

230
00:08:11,146 --> 00:08:12,178
million.

231
00:08:12,417 --> 00:08:14,530
The commission's data also showed

232
00:08:14,530 --> 00:08:16,187
something telling.

233
00:08:16,247 --> 00:08:18,511
In early twenty twenty-six,

234
00:08:18,511 --> 00:08:20,545
the value of deals with entities

235
00:08:20,545 --> 00:08:22,809
associated with the big schools,

236
00:08:22,809 --> 00:08:24,309
often booster groups,

237
00:08:24,309 --> 00:08:26,598
rose by three hundred and sixty-four

238
00:08:26,598 --> 00:08:27,707
percent.

239
00:08:27,967 --> 00:08:30,076
The line between a sponsorship and a

240
00:08:30,076 --> 00:08:32,607
disguised salary is exactly what the

241
00:08:32,607 --> 00:08:34,894
referee is trying to police.

242
00:08:35,150 --> 00:08:37,840
Let me draw the checkpoint.

243
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:40,310
Here comes a line of deals.

244
00:08:40,310 --> 00:08:42,380
Most of them pass through.

245
00:08:42,410 --> 00:08:44,180
A few are stopped.

246
00:08:44,450 --> 00:08:45,890
By our calculation,

247
00:08:45,890 --> 00:08:48,186
almost one in every five dollars of

248
00:08:48,186 --> 00:08:50,065
the deals reviewed was in a deal

249
00:08:50,065 --> 00:08:51,500
that did not clear.

250
00:08:51,610 --> 00:08:53,803
The checkpoint is where the money meets

251
00:08:53,803 --> 00:08:55,135
the rules.

252
00:08:55,383 --> 00:08:58,523
Which leaves the biggest question of all.

253
00:08:58,603 --> 00:09:00,765
Are these athletes students,

254
00:09:00,765 --> 00:09:02,186
or employees?

255
00:09:07,850 --> 00:09:09,566
In twenty twenty-four,

256
00:09:09,566 --> 00:09:12,302
a federal appeals court ruled in a

257
00:09:12,302 --> 00:09:15,140
case called Johnson versus the NCAA.

258
00:09:15,550 --> 00:09:17,974
It said athletes cannot be barred from

259
00:09:17,974 --> 00:09:19,958
claiming wages just because they are

260
00:09:19,958 --> 00:09:21,660
called amateurs.

261
00:09:21,810 --> 00:09:24,457
Courts must look at the economic reality

262
00:09:24,457 --> 00:09:25,940
of what they do.

263
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:27,686
The court sent the case back to

264
00:09:27,686 --> 00:09:30,217
a lower court to do exactly that,

265
00:09:30,217 --> 00:09:32,675
and the question is still open.

266
00:09:32,917 --> 00:09:36,127
Washington is trying to write the rules.

267
00:09:36,217 --> 00:09:38,200
In April twenty twenty-six,

268
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:41,097
the President signed an executive order

269
00:09:41,097 --> 00:09:43,549
asking for revenue sharing that preserves

270
00:09:43,549 --> 00:09:46,377
or expands women's and Olympic sports.

271
00:09:46,607 --> 00:09:49,597
Its main sections took effect in August.

272
00:09:49,807 --> 00:09:52,394
An executive order can push the schools,

273
00:09:52,394 --> 00:09:54,785
but it cannot rewrite the settlement or

274
00:09:54,785 --> 00:09:56,907
the labor laws on its own.

275
00:09:57,187 --> 00:09:58,302
In September,

276
00:09:58,302 --> 00:10:00,578
the Senate passed a bill called the

277
00:10:00,578 --> 00:10:02,149
Protect College Sports Act,

278
00:10:02,149 --> 00:10:04,797
seventy-seven to twenty-two.

279
00:10:05,057 --> 00:10:07,040
The House has not voted on it,

280
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:08,755
so it is not law.

281
00:10:09,017 --> 00:10:11,677
Let me draw the two doors.

282
00:10:11,817 --> 00:10:13,532
One door says student,

283
00:10:13,532 --> 00:10:16,167
with scholarships and classes.

284
00:10:16,227 --> 00:10:18,211
The other says employee,

285
00:10:18,211 --> 00:10:20,537
with wages and contracts.

286
00:10:20,727 --> 00:10:23,222
Today's athletes stand in the hallway

287
00:10:23,222 --> 00:10:24,195
between them,

288
00:10:24,195 --> 00:10:26,190
with a payroll in one hand and

289
00:10:26,190 --> 00:10:28,786
a student ID in the other.

290
00:10:29,050 --> 00:10:31,505
So how did college sports get a

291
00:10:31,505 --> 00:10:34,560
twenty million dollar payroll?

